    <li>country</li>
    <li>family</li>
    <li>timing</li>
    <li>connections</li>
  </ul>
</section>

It is too complex, isn't it?

<!--
  REMARK(beats 3+4 MERGED): this joke beat now carries the load-bearing inference that
  outline.md beat 4 wanted ("if the simplest version can't escape it, no richer version
  can"). The old beat-4 TODO is resolved by the uphill analogy below — shape = biography we
  discard; weight = the mechanism we can't. Honesty guard: the analogy only claims that an
  obstacle present in the ideal case survives into messier cases. It does NOT claim every
  richer model behaves identically. Flow change logged in notes/draft-decisions.md (D3).
-->

## Let me tell you a joke

<!--
  STAGE — there is one canvas, in its own fixed place. It holds the picture and MORPHS in place
  as these words arrive and leave; it does NOT scroll with the text. The through-line is the
  circle: a cow reduces to it, then a person reduces to the same circle, then the circle grows
  with money. Nothing here is fixed in code — the `CANVAS →` notes are soft writing cues, not a
  component spec. Whole-essay writing stance is recorded in notes/draft-decisions.md (D8).
-->
<figure class="stage" aria-label="An illustration that simplifies a cow, then a person, into a circle, and then grows it">[ stage ]</figure>

A farmer's cow stops giving milk. So he calls a physicist.

<!-- CANVAS → a cow, drawn plainly. -->

She studies it for a long time. Then:

<aside class="claim-note">
  <p><strong>"First, assume a spherical cow."</strong></p>
</aside>

<!-- CANVAS → the cow rounds into a perfect sphere. -->

<aside class="claim-note">
  <p><strong>"In a vacuum."</strong></p>
</aside>

<!-- CANVAS → the air is pulled out; the sphere holds, alone in the empty. -->

Ridiculous. Which is why it has lasted a hundred years.

It is also the best trick in science: throw away every detail but one, and ask if that one
is enough.

And here is the catch that makes it honest. A spherical cow is the *easiest* cow to push
uphill — no friction, no legs in the way. So if even it won't move, a square cow won't
move, and a real cow won't either.

<!-- CANVAS → the sphere sits on a slope and will not go up; a square cow, then a real cow, try in turn — both slide back. -->

The problem was never the shape. It was the weight.


## Now, the spherical human

Same trick. New subject.

<!--
  REMARK(beat 5 via shared canvas): the Musk→circle morph happens ON THE SAME canvas as the cow
  — the place stays, the occupant changes. Causal-claim guard kept in the words ("we are not
  going to explain how he got it"); brief §4. Logged in notes/draft-decisions.md (D5).
-->
<!-- CANVAS → where the cow was, a recognisable cartoon of the trillionaire from the opening fades in. -->

Take the most-photographed fortune on earth. We are not going to explain how he got it. We
are going to do to him exactly what the physicist did to the cow.

First, assume a spherical human. In a vacuum.

Throw away the companies, the employees, the investors, the contracts, the courts, the
timing, the inheritance, the talent, the taste, the enemies, the luck — every argument we
love to have about whether any of it was earned.

<!-- CANVAS → with each thing named, a piece of him drops away — clothes, face, body — down to the same circle the cow became. -->

What is left is almost offensively small:

<!-- REMARK(reduction line): authored, crude, UNATTRIBUTED; "money" not "net worth" (jargon). See notes/draft-decisions.md D6. -->
<aside class="claim-note">
  <p><strong>You are your money.</strong></p>
</aside>

I don't believe that sentence. I want it to feel wrong. The wrongness is the point.

We are not describing a human being. We are building the smallest possible room: everyone
starts equal, every trade is fair, and the only thing a person *is*, is a number.

If even that room fills with billionaires and paupers, then the weight was never the shape
of the person. It was the math.


## Then we add the money

So: a circle. That is the person now. Give it a coin —

<!-- CANVAS → a coin drops into the circle and it grows; area ∝ wealth (radius = scale·√wealth), so the eye reads size honestly (Cleveland/McGill, narrative-sources.md). -->

— and it grows.

The size is the money: the area, not the width. A circle twice as wide is four times as
rich. Big circle, big fortune. That is the whole language.

<!-- TEST(Study A, reader-study.md): can readers rank circles, read the direction of a change, and judge rough ratios from area alone, without colour? Revisit if area is misread as radius. -->

Now there is not one circle but a roomful, every one the same size — same circle, same
coin, same chance.

<!-- CANVAS → the single circle multiplies into a room of identical circles. From here the stage's occupant becomes the interactive simulation: the authored morph hands over. -->

Then they begin to trade.

## One trade

Pick two of them at random. Any two.

<!--
  WIDGET: ENCOUNTER — the room of circles; two are picked uniformly at random and pulled into
  focus. Interactive sim slot (mount/config/events/cleanup per widget contract).
-->
<figure class="sim-stage" data-stage="encounter" aria-label="Two circles picked at random from the room">[ two circles ]</figure>

They put something on the table. Not everything — a fixed slice of whatever the *poorer*
one owns. So no one can ever stake more than they have.

Then they flip a coin.

<aside class="claim-note">
  <p><strong>Heads, the first one takes the slice. Tails, the second.</strong></p>
</aside>

Nothing else decides it. Not who is bigger. Not who is smarter. Not who "deserves" it. A
fair coin, every time.

And *fair* here means one exact thing: before the flip, neither one expects to come out
ahead. The winner gains exactly what the loser loses. No money is made. None is destroyed.
It only moves.

That is the whole game.

## Watch a few

Let me run a few by hand — I will pick the outcomes, just to show you the rule.

<!--
  WIDGET: SCRIPTED — authored (non-random) sequence: one wins, loses it back, a few more, and
  a return to roughly equal. Must read as instruction, not a random run (label it as such).
-->
<figure class="sim-stage" data-stage="scripted" aria-label="A few scripted trades that return to equal">[ scripted trades ]</figure>

One wins, and pulls ahead. Next trade, it loses the slice straight back. Even again. A few
more, back and forth — and look, everyone is roughly where they started.

See? Symmetric. Win some, lose some. The money sloshes around and settles. Nothing runs
away. How could it? The coin is fair.

Hold on to that feeling. It is about to betray you.

## Your turn to guess

Here is the setup, clean:

- everyone starts with the same circle;
- every trade picks a random pair;
- every coin is fair, fifty-fifty.

Now let it run. Not five trades. Ten thousand.

<!--
  WIDGET: PREDICTION — let the reader commit to an outcome before the reveal (e.g. pick a rough
  shape for the room). Their guess is the setup for the reveal below; log it if cheap.
-->
<figure class="sim-stage" data-stage="predict" aria-label="Predict the room after ten thousand trades">[ your guess ]</figure>

What does the room look like after ten thousand fair trades? Still roughly equal? A little
spread? Something else? Pick one before you scroll.

## Now run it

<!--
  WIDGET: MAIN REVEAL — the real Yard-Sale rule, tuned preset, NO user controls. Run to a
  visibly concentrated state: one circle enormous, the rest near zero. Seeded / reproducible.
  Claim discipline: describe the finite run only — NO power-law or distribution-family claim
  here (that is gated, Chapter III). Convergence-to-one-owner is the asymptotic beat 17, not this.
-->
<figure class="sim-stage" data-stage="reveal" aria-label="Ten thousand fair trades; one circle grows enormous">[ the run ]</figure>

One of them is enormous. The rest are dust.

Every trade was fair — I just showed you. No coin was loaded. Nobody cheated. Nobody was
smarter. And still: one circle ate the room.

The trade was fair. The result is not. Those are two different things, and the whole essay
lives in the gap between them.

## Don't believe it? Run it again.

<!--
  WIDGET: RE-RUN — new seed, same preset; a different agent wins. Silently log each run's end
  state to build the histogram in beat 14 (Chapter III).
-->
<figure class="sim-stage" data-stage="rerun" aria-label="Run again; a different winner, same concentration">[ run again ]</figure>

Different winner. Same ending.

Run it again. And again. The crown lands on someone new every time — but it always lands.
The winner is random. The winner-take-all is not.

## So why did they win?

The room has its winner. The next morning, the news explains them.

<!--
  WIDGET: WINNER HEADLINE — generate a news headline that crowns the winner, built from their
  single most visually distinctive (pre-assigned, arbitrary) trait. Reuses the opening
  `headline-card` motif ON PURPOSE: the essay opened on a real headline; now we manufacture one
  about a random circle. The headline text + source line are widget-filled from the winner.
  [TEST] reader-study Study D (research/reader-study.md).
  GUARD (critical failure mode + widget contract): traits are assigned BEFORE the run and must
  NEVER enter pair selection, the stake, or the coin. The sim core must not read them.
  Do NOT name a cognitive bias in prose (research.md C12).
-->
<div class="headline-card winner-headline">
  <p class="headline-text">Squares: the shape of success</p>
  <p class="headline-source">How one sharp-cornered trader out-played the room</p>
</div>

Look at them. A square, in a room full of circles. Sharper. More decisive. Built to win.
The story writes itself.

And it is a lie. That trait — being a square — never touched the game. Not the coin, not
who got picked, not the stake. I handed out the shapes at random before the first trade,
and not one of them changed a single outcome.

<!--
  WIDGET: RE-RUN HEADLINE — run again; a different winner, a different distinctive trait, a new
  headline written with the same confidence. The story is always selected after the result.
-->
<div class="headline-card winner-headline">
  <p class="headline-text">[ new winner, new "winning trait" ]</p>
  <p class="headline-source">[ an equally confident explanation ]</p>
</div>

Same room. New winner. New headline, just as convincing.

Remember the headline we started with?

Watch how easily a result invites a story.

## Line them up

A hundred circles, all different sizes, scattered around the room. You cannot read that.
Nobody can.

So sort them. Smallest to largest, and drop each one into a bin — a pile for the near-broke,
a pile for the comfortable, a pile for the rich.

<!-- CANVAS → the scattered circles slide into stacks by size; the stacks square off into bars. The histogram is built FROM the same circles the reader watched trade. -->

Now it is a shape: a chart whose bars are the people you just watched. (Where I cut the
lines between the piles is a choice, not nature's — move them and the picture shifts a
little.)

And the shape leans hard. A crowd of the poor piled up on one side, a thin tail reaching
out the other — and far past everyone, alone, the winner.

<!-- TEST(Study B, reader-study.md): histogram vs cumulative/Lorenz as the first distribution view; choose by comprehension, not familiarity. -->

## The picture is broken

There is a problem with that chart. The winner is so large that, to fit them on the screen,
everyone else gets crushed into one thin sliver at the bottom. Every difference among the
people who actually lost — gone.

<!-- CANVAS → linear axis: the poor collapse into a single sliver; the winner shoots off the edge. -->

So change the ruler. Instead of each step *adding* the same amount, each step *multiplies*:
1, 10, 100, 1,000. The same distance across the screen now means ten times the money.

<!-- CANVAS → the axis re-rules itself; circles glide from their linear spots to logarithmic ones; the crushed sliver opens into a readable spread. -->

Now you can see all of them at once. And the gap between two circles stops telling you how
much more one has. It tells you how many *times* more.

(One honest catch: you cannot multiply your way up from zero, so a circle with nothing has
no spot on this ruler. The truly empty sit in their own pile, off to the side.)

<!--
  GATE(no power law): the log view only reveals values a linear scale hid. It does NOT show or
  prove a power-law / heavy-tailed distribution family — omit "power law" from the baseline arc
  entirely (research.md; narrative-sources.md log note). A fit + goodness-of-fit + model
  comparison would be required first.
  TEST(Study C, reader-study.md): does a side-by-side linear↔log transform plus a comprehension
  check leave readers able to read ratios? A bare label is not enough (Ryan & Evers).
-->

## More of them

You have seen it with a handful. Watch it with a crowd.

<!-- CANVAS → a much larger seeded population runs the same fair trades; the log-ruled histogram forms again. -->

Same rules. Same fair coin. Many more people. Read it yourself — the wall of the poor, the
thinning tail, the one circle out past the edge. The crowd does not soften the result. It
sharpens it.

## Where it ends

So where does this go, if no one ever stops it?

This part is a theorem, not just an animation. Keep the room finite. Keep every trade fair,
every coin even. Then let it run — not ten thousand trades, but without end.

<!-- CANVAS → time accelerates; the tail thins and thins until one circle holds everything and the rest are dust. -->

With probability one, it ends with a single owner. Everyone else goes to zero. A different
winner each time you run it — but always, in the end, exactly one.

Two cautions, because they carry the honesty of the whole piece:

- "Without end" is doing the work. This is the limit, not a date. For any real number of
  trades you get deep concentration — not literally one owner. The time-lapse shows a
  destination, not a deadline.
- The path can wobble. A poorer circle can win, and the room can briefly grow fairer, even
  as the whole process grinds toward the one.

<aside class="claim-note">
  <p>The finite-agent convergence result is proved in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.01485">Börgers &amp; Greengard (2023)</a>.</p>
</aside>

## Your hand on the dial

So that is the game as written: fair coin, fixed stake, one ending.

Now you get a knob.

The stake — the slice each trade puts on the table — has been fixed this whole time. Take
hold of it. Turn it down, and each trade risks almost nothing. Turn it up, and each trade is
a gamble for a real chunk.

<!-- CANVAS → a stake control appears on the room; dragging it re-runs the room live at the chosen stake. -->

Watch what changes — and what refuses to.

Turn the stake down and the collapse drags: the room looks fair for a long, comfortable
while. Turn it up and the winner arrives fast and brutal. The speed is yours.

The ending is not. Any stake above nothing, given enough time, still empties into a single
circle. You can change how fast the room falls. You do not get to vote on whether it falls.

<!--
  GATE(β timing): the qualitative split is safe — asymptotic concentration is the same for every
  fixed β in (0,1) (C4); the *rate* is shown as what the live runs do, not a precise law. Do NOT
  state a precise timing relationship (e.g. a formula in β or N) until the confirmatory grid
  across N, β, seeds lands (research/baseline.md). Keep rate language observational.
-->

(Set the stake to exactly zero and the room freezes — but only because then nobody is
trading at all. Nothing at stake, nothing happens.)

## Now you try to stop it

Reading about it is one thing. Try to fix it yourself.

Here is a live room, falling the usual way. Your job: keep it from collapsing. Your tool is
blunt and satisfying — point at a circle that has grown too fat, and tax it. Whatever you
pull out is shared back across the room.

<!--
  CANVAS → the live room keeps trading while the reader taps; tapping a large circle siphons
  wealth from it and sprinkles it across the rest; the tapped circle flinches (display affect).
  This is a manual WEALTH levy (taxes current wealth, not income) — name the quantity, C13.
  DESIGN GUARD: do NOT tune this so the reader provably cannot win, then present that as a
  result. Let them feel it; whether a fast hand can hold a room is a real, open question
  (ideas.md tax-the-rich; outline beat 19). Frame stays "inside this toy," never real tax policy.
-->
<figure class="sim-stage" data-stage="manual-tax" aria-label="A live room you can tax by tapping the largest circles">[ tax the rich ]</figure>

Go ahead. Chase the big ones. Tap, tap, tap.

Notice how it feels. The room keeps trading while you work — you tax one giant, and two more
are already swelling behind your back. It is like bailing a boat that refills itself.

Maybe you can hold it. Maybe you cannot. That is genuinely worth finding out, so I will not
pretend to know the answer for you: can a fast enough hand keep a room fair?

<!--
  OPEN(outline beat 19): where the taxed wealth goes is an unresolved design fork — split evenly
  to everyone vs. handed to whoever is poorest. Different mechanisms, different lessons. The
  parenthetical below flags it without deciding; resolve when the widget is specified.
-->
(There is a quieter question hiding in here, too: the money you take — should it be split
evenly across everyone, or handed straight to whoever has least? Those are different choices,
and they teach different lessons. Hold that thought.)

