## What I left out

Before the ending, the honesty bill.

This room is almost nothing like an economy. To build it, I threw away — and never handed
back — work, wages, jobs, the things people actually make and sell. Debt. Saving.
Inheritance. Prices. Skill. Effort. Luck. Power. Law. The thousand ways real people climb,
fall, cheat, help one another, and start over.

<!-- CANVAS → everything stripped away in the opening floods back onto the lone circle — the labels, the biography, the cartoon person re-forming around it. The reverse of the opening morph. -->

That list is not an apology. It is the fence around the claim. A model this bare cannot tell
you what causes real inequality, and it never tried to. It shows one thing, cleanly, with
everything else taken out of the way.

## So — merit, or math?

We began with a person and a number. A headline. The first trillionaire.

<!-- CANVAS → the re-formed person finishes morphing back into the opening headline card, closing the loop. -->

I cannot tell you how he got there. This little room does not know his name, and neither of
us should pretend it does.

But here is what the room *does* show, and it is smaller and harder than any headline:

A process can be fair in every single step and still end with everything in one place. Local
fairness does not add up to a fair whole. Nobody has to cheat. Equal starts do not promise
equal ends.

So the next time a fortune crosses some impossible line and the stories arrive to explain it
— the talent, the grind, the vision — keep one cold possibility in the back of your mind.
Maybe all of that is real. Or maybe you are reading a winning trait off a fair coin.

There was no loaded coin here. No villain in the code. No conspiracy. Every trade was fair.

<aside class="claim-note">
  <p><strong>Nothing was unfair. That was the unfairness.</strong></p>
</aside>

